Sound Dimension  As [former Apple marketing executive Frederica] Woods spins the tale, it was just weeks before iMac's first Christmas in 1998 when slowing holiday sales rang the alarm in the CEO's office. Steve Jobs, a lifelong fan of James Bond (he'd originally wanted to name the revolutionary computer \"\"Double-O-Mac\"\"), instructed his agency to begin work on a special celebrity Christmas ad featuring 007 himself, Sean Connery — even though Connery had yet to be signed. \"\"The ad was of dubious quality, clearly not one of the agency's finer moments,\"\" says Woods.  Pingback: Quick Look: l’iMac di 007 - TheAppleLounge Pingback: James Bond Tears Steve Jobs a New One Over iMac Ad - 1998 | THE SILVER THREAD Pingback: Carta de Sean Connery a Steve Jobs — Tecnoculto Pingback: Sean Connery csúnyán beszólt az Apple-nek? | Urban Legends  First, the bad news. Sean Connery never actually sent a typewritten letter to Steve Jobs in 1998 refusing to be in an Apple ad. But the awesome news is that quite a few people believe Connery owns personalized stationery with a \"\"007\"\" vanity stamp in the corner and that he would have no qualms using it to dash off a letter dressing down Jobs by declaring \"\"...you are a computer salesman. The letter was actually part of a satirical article on the previously little known (and very specific) humor site, Scoopertino, which peddles Onion-style and tongue-in-cheek \"\"Unreal Apple News.\"\"  Faux 007 launching expletives at Apple icon fools British social-media guru and becomes meme material. First, the bad news. Sean Connery never actually sent a typewritten letter to Steve Jobs in 1998 refusing to be in an Apple ad. But the awesome news is that quite a few people believe Connery owns personalized stationery with a \"\"007\"\" vanity stamp in the corner and that he would have no qualms using it to dash off a letter dressing down Jobs by declaring \"\"...you are a computer salesman.  actually, you just need to look at image of the ad. Check out the very bottom. Well, in that era, Apple put out ads that featured Alfred Hitchcock, John & Yoko and Muhammad Ali so it's probably safe to assume that Apple tried to snag Sean Connery but failed. The letter though is fake but hilarious nonetheless, up for you @SE20! \t\t\n\nWas there a letter written to Steve Jobs by Sean Connery refusing to appear in an Apple commercial?\t\t\nThis is a letter Sean Connery wrote didn't write in response to Steve Jobs after being asked to appear in an Apple ad. Honestly, we're not sure it's legit. The \"\"007\"\" at the bottom-right corner and the \"\"I am fucking JAMES BOND\"\" seem a bit rich.  C’est un courrier qui est devenu viral en 2011. Une simple lettre adressée par Sean Connery à Steve Jobs le 11 décembre 1998, dans laquelle l’acteur ayant incarné l’agent secret James Bond de 1962 à 1971, priait Steve Jobs de ne plus insister pour le voir apparaître dans une publicité de la marque (on pense évidemment à la campagne Think Bien évidemment, il s’agit d’un faux. Imagine-t-on vraiment Sean Connery écrire sur du papier à lettre portant le logo 007, et rétorquer à Steve Jobs, s’il comprend l’anglais, qu’il ne le répétera pas : il n’est pas intéressé pour « changer le monde » et ne vendra pas son âme à Apple ni à quelqu’autre entreprise.  First, the bad news. Sean Connery never actually sent a typewritten letter to Steve Jobs in 1998 refusing to be in an Apple ad. But the awesome news is that quite a few people believe Connery owns personalized stationery with a \"\"007\"\" vanity stamp in the corner and that he would have no qualms using it to dash off a letter dressing down Jobs by declaring \"\"...you are a computer salesman. The letter was actually part of a satirical article on the previously little known (and very specific) humor site, Scoopertino, which peddles Onion-style and tongue-in-cheek \"\"Unreal Apple News.\"\"  Faux 007 launching expletives at Apple icon fools British social-media guru and becomes meme 